        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Page 3",
      "pageStart": 3,
      "pageEnd": 3,
      "target": "../sections/page-003.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "1.1 EXECUTIVE SUMMARY\nNew HIV infections in children continue to occur globally \nand timely diagnosis and treatment of infants and children \nliving with HIV remain critically important. The 2016 WHO \nARV Consolidated Guidelines presented some innovative \napproaches, such as the use of nucleic acid testing (NAT) \nat or around birth for earlier diagnosis of HIV in infants, \nthe introduction of point-of-care (POC) NAT for more rapid \nand decentralized diagnosis to enable prompt antiretroviral \ntherapy (ART) initiation, and the use of enhanced \npostnatal prophylaxis (ePNP) to improve HIV preventio",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Gives the executive summary, explaining that few countries have adopted the 2016 WHO innovations of birth testing, point-of-care NAT and enhanced postnatal prophylaxis, and that this update draws on 2017 and 2018 expert meetings to describe changing strategies for infant HIV. It is followed by a list of abbreviations.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-273155/blocks/prose-page-004",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Page 4",
      "pageStart": 4,
      "pageEnd": 4,
      "target": "../sections/page-004.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "2.1 BACKGROUND\n2.1.1 Current status quo regarding pediatric HIV\nThe Global Plan Towards the Elimination of New HIV \nInfections among Children by 2015 and Keeping their \nMothers Alive initiative has had a substantial impact, leading \nto a 60% reduction in new pediatric HIV infections in 21 \nhigh-burden countries in sub-Saharan Africa.1,2 Nevertheless, \nthe burden of new HIV infections in children remains \nsignificant: in 2017, there were 180 000 new infections in \nchildren globally, and 70% of these children were in the \nsame 21 priority countries. The Start Free, Stay Free, AIDS \nFree framewor",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes the status of paediatric HIV, noting the drop in new child infections under the Global Plan but 180 000 new infections in 2017, and the shift of mother-to-child transmission towards the postnatal period. A figure shows early and later transmission rates in the priority countries of the Start Free, Stay Free, AIDS Free framework.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-273155/blocks/prose-page-005",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Page 5",
      "pageStart": 5,
      "pageEnd": 5,
      "target": "../sections/page-005.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Furthermore, although pediatric ART coverage has notably \nimproved since 2010, only 51% of the estimated 1.8 \nmillion children living with HIV were receiving ART by the \nend of 2017.3 HIV-infected infants and younger children \nhave an exceptionally high mortality without treatment, \napproximately 30% by the first year and 50% by their \nsecond year of life.9 Many HIV-related deaths in infants \ncan be avoided by early identification of HIV and rapid \nART initiation. Limited availability of optimal antiretroviral \n(ARV) formulations for preventing and treating HIV \ninfection in newborn and young",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Notes that only about half of children living with HIV were on treatment in 2017 and that untreated infants face very high mortality, then sets out the reasons for this technical update. It explains that falling transmission rates and low viremia have reduced the predictive value of nucleic acid tests, so false-positive infant diagnoses are increasingly reported. The page raises the question of introducing an indeterminate result range for early infant diagnosis.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
