    {
      "id": "library/10665-273155/blocks/prose-page-006",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Page 6",
      "pageStart": 6,
      "pageEnd": 6,
      "target": "../sections/page-006.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "A systematic review of 32 studies using an indeterminate \nrange found 14 753 non-negative test results of which \n2 436 (16.5% [95% CI 15.9–17.1%]) were indeterminate \n(data unpublished); one study14 that reported the final \ndiagnoses of indeterminate cases found that 76% of infants \nwith an initial indeterminate test result were negative \non repeat testing, suggesting these infants were not \nHIV-infected despite the initial non-negative test result. \nThese data indicate that in countries not implementing an \nindeterminate range for NAT and where MCTC rates are \nlow (<5%), 12.5% (76% of 16.5%)",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Reports a systematic review indicating that a sizeable share of non-negative infant test results are indeterminate, most of which prove negative on retesting, and explains the use of PCR cycle thresholds to define an indeterminate range. It closes with definitions of terms such as indeterminate, non-negative and discordant results.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-273155/blocks/prose-page-007",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Page 7",
      "pageStart": 7,
      "pageEnd": 7,
      "target": "../sections/page-007.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "period and all infants with a positive result receive \na confirmatory test. Furthermore, those with repeatedly \nindeterminate test results should be actively tracked, \nretained, retested and their status resolved.\nFinally, POC EID testing is being implemented in several \ncountries and settings (see 3.1.3). Previously there was \nlimited evidence on how to conduct confirmatory testing of \nPOC EID positive test results, but since publication of the \n2016 WHO ARV Consolidated Guidelines10 several studies \nhave been published on its performance. Two POC EID \ntechnologies are now included on the WHO",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Discusses confirmatory testing, noting that prequalified point-of-care infant diagnosis devices perform comparably to laboratory tests and can be used to confirm positive results, and covers management of discordant results and treatment interruption. A summary box stresses immediate retesting of indeterminate results, team support for repeated cases and confirmatory testing of all positives.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-273155/blocks/prose-page-008",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Page 8",
      "pageStart": 8,
      "pageEnd": 8,
      "target": "../sections/page-008.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "3.1.3 Implementation of POC EID testing\nThe 2016 WHO ARV Consolidated Guidelines10 recommend \nthe use of NAT technologies for early infant HIV testing \nthat have been developed and validated for use at or near \nthe point-of-care. POC EID provides the opportunity to \nreduce test turnaround times, limit patient loss along the \nHIV testing cascade, reduce infant mortality and facilitate \ntask shifting to lower cadres of health workers at health-\ncare facilities with decentralized services. Sufficient \nevidence has been generated on the performance of these \nassays in their intended field settings",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Covers the rollout of point-of-care early infant diagnosis, citing studies in Malawi and Mozambique showing faster results and earlier treatment, and lists lessons on device placement and site selection. It also addresses birth testing, and a box urges national regulators to approve the two prequalified technologies quickly.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-273155/blocks/prose-page-009",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Page 9",
      "pageStart": 9,
      "pageEnd": 9,
      "target": "../sections/page-009.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "•\t Birth testing is acceptable to mothers, but challenges \narise from the increased human resources needed, the \ndifficulty of collecting blood samples in newborns, the \nneed to ensure sample collection outside of standard \nworking hours and deliver results, linkage to ART and \nthe nature of the EID system as a whole (stock-outs, \nreferral mechanisms, delayed results).\nBox 4. Implementation of birth testing: \ncountry experiences\nSouth Africa\nSouth Africa introduced NAT at birth in 2015 with \nspecific nurse training at postnatal and delivery services \nand health register updates to enable data",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Summarizes challenges of HIV testing at birth, such as staffing, sampling newborns and linkage to treatment, and describes country experiences in South Africa and Kenya. It stresses linking infected newborns to treatment with suitable formulations and the need for good leadership and coordination.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
