        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Page 11",
      "pageStart": 11,
      "pageEnd": 11,
      "target": "../sections/page-011.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "3.1.5 Ensuring accurate interpretation of the 9-month \ntest and simplifying the testing algorithm \nThe 2016 WHO ARV Consolidated Guidelines recommend \nthat RDTs should be used to assess HIV exposure among \ninfants younger than 4 months, while HIV exposure \namong infants 4–18 months old should be ascertained \nby testing the mother. When testing of the mother is not \npossible, current guidelines emphasize the importance \nof not considering a negative RDT result from an infant \nbetween 4–18 months as a definitive test of exposure. \nImplementation issues are highlighted in Box 5.\nBased on the 2016",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Addresses interpretation of the 9-month infant test and simplification of the testing algorithm, explaining how maternal treatment, infant prophylaxis and late maternal infection can make rapid antibody tests unreliable for exposure or infection status. It sets out key points: testing mothers to establish exposure, not treating a negative infant result after 4 months as definitive, and confirming positive results with NAT.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-273155/blocks/prose-page-012",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Page 12",
      "pageStart": 12,
      "pageEnd": 12,
      "target": "../sections/page-012.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "3.2. ARV use for prevention and treatment \nof HIV in infants\n3.2.1 Implementation challenges to providing ePNP for \nhigh-risk infants\nThe 2016 WHO ARV Consolidated Guidelines recommend \na dual regimen of AZT and NVP which can be extended \nfor up to 12 weeks in breastfeeding infants deemed to be \nat high risk of MTCT. A high-risk infant is defined as an \ninfant whose mother was first identified as HIV-infected at \ndelivery or in the postpartum, infected during pregnancy \nor breastfeeding, started ART late in pregnancy, or did not \nachieve viral suppression by the time of delivery (Annex 4). \nAl",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains the WHO recommendation of dual AZT and nevirapine prophylaxis for high-risk infants, extended to 12 weeks in breastfeeding infants, and defines which infants are high risk. It discusses the rationale based on mothers reaching viral suppression and gives guidance on whether to continue infant prophylaxis when maternal adherence is poor. It advises that prophylaxis, once stopped, should not be restarted and that attention should go to the causes of poor maternal adherence.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-273155/blocks/prose-page-013",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Page 13",
      "pageStart": 13,
      "pageEnd": 13,
      "target": "../sections/page-013.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "As more effective treatments increasingly come to be used \nin pregnant women, we can expect decreasing MTCT rates. \nHowever, some women will still be diagnosed late or have \nincident infections and this group is likely to drive most \nnew cases of HIV transmission to infants, owing to the high \nlevel of maternal virus in the absence of treatment. One \npossibility would be to provide “presumptive treatment” \nas postnatal ePNP, by administering a triple-drug regimen \nat therapeutic doses to this selected group of infants with \nthe goal of minimizing transmission and drug-resistant \nHIV (HIVDR) se",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Considers giving high-risk infants presumptive triple-drug treatment as enhanced prophylaxis, paired with testing before it starts, and describes the limited and complex options for early infant treatment. It weighs the use of fixed-dose combination tablets, noting issues such as unwanted lamivudine exposure and the need to split tablets into quarters.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
