      "target": "../sections/page-014.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "provide opportunities to strengthen service delivery for \nHIV-exposed infants. Integration within a well established \nmaternal, neonatal, and child services platform, which \ntraditionally provides services closest to clients, facilitates \nmother-infant pair follow-up and reduces the cost and \ntime-visit burden on clients. The integrated information \nsystems that link mother and infant information improve \nclient tracking and facilitate continuity of care provision. \nExamples include longitudinal follow-up registers and \ncohort analysis as well as linkage with information on \ncommunit-based ser",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Discusses integrating care for HIV-exposed infants into maternal and child health services and linked information systems, while noting pressure on staff. It also describes the role of community services and networks of women living with HIV, and lists patient-level and health system interventions to keep infants in the testing cascade.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
